  "output_hash": "cf195a9d6f91187ac99403b0777d44d33db93e8e0a041de8064efaab14a12d14",
  "output": {
    "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
    "points": [
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p001",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "definition",
        "importance": 2,
        "text": "Fine-tuning is adapting a pre-trained model to a specific task by training the model on additional data.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@19",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@21",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@24",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@27"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p002",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 2,
        "text": "There are two types of fine-tuning discussed here: instruction fine-tuning and classification fine-tuning.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@31",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@32",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@34",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@37"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p003",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "The classification fine-tuning project in this lecture series uses a large language model to classify emails as either spam or not spam.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@48",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@51",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@55",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@57",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@60",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@63",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@64",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@67",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@70"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p004",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 2,
        "text": "The email dataset came from the UCI Irvine Machine Learning Repository and contained about 747 spam emails and more than 3,000 non-spam emails, where non-spam is also called ham.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@89",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@91",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@93",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@96",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@97",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@101",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@105",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@107",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@109"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p005",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 2,
        "text": "Each dataset entry consists of a text and a label indicating ham or spam.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@118",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@120",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@124",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@126",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@129",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@131"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p006",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 2,
        "text": "The lecturer balanced the dataset by randomly sampling 747 non-spam entries so that both spam and non-spam had 747 examples.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@135",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@138",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@141",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@144",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@147",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@150",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@152"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p007",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "The labels were encoded numerically with ham as 0 and spam as 1.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@154",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@157",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@160"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p008",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 2,
        "text": "The dataset was split into 70% training data, 10% validation data, and 20% testing data, and these splits were saved as CSV files.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@163",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@165",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@168",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@171",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@174",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@176",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@180",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@183",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@185",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@187",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@189"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p009",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "For classification fine-tuning, the model takes input text and outputs whether the email is spam or not spam.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@192",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@193",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@195",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@198",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@200",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@204",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@207",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@210",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@212"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p010",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "definition",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "The purpose of the data loader is to feed input-target pairs into the model and manage the data more effectively.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@221",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@223",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@227",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@229",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@232",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@237",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@239",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@280",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@282",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@284",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@288",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@289"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p011",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 2,
        "text": "In PyTorch, Dataset and DataLoader can be used with a custom dataset to access samples easily, run batches, and do parallel processing.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@242",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@244",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@248",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@250",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@253",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@258",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@261",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@264",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@266",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@269",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@271",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@273"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p012",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "The goal is to convert each email into tokens so that every email in a batch has the same token length, producing an input tensor and a corresponding target tensor of labels 0 or 1.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@293",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@295",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@298",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@302",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@305",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@307",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@309",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@311",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@313",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@316",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@322",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@325",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@327",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@329",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@332",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@334",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@336",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@338",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@340",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@343",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@345",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@348",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@350",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@352"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p013",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "A key issue is that email texts have varying lengths, but batching requires every row in the batch to have the same number of columns.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@367",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@370",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@373",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@376",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@378",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@380",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@383",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@386",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@388",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@390",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@392",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@395",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@396",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@399",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@400",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@403",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@405"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p014",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "procedure",
        "importance": 2,
        "text": "One possible way to equalize sequence lengths is to find the shortest email and truncate all longer emails to that length.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@405",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@407",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@410",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@412",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@414",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@417",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@420",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@423",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@425",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@426",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@428",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@431",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@434",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@437",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@438",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@440"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p015",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "Truncating all emails to the shortest length is not recommended because it loses information from longer texts.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@443",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@445",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@447",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@449",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@451",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@454",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@457",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@460",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@463",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@465"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p016",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "procedure",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "A better approach is to use the longest email length and pad all shorter emails with additional tokens until they reach that length.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@467",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@469",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@472",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@475",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@481",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@484",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@488",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@490",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@494",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@495",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@498",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@499",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@502",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@503",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@505"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p017",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "procedure",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "The padding token used here is the end-of-text token.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@507",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@509",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@511",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@516"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p018",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "procedure",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "The first step in preprocessing is to tokenize each text message.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@529",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@531"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p019",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "definition",
        "importance": 2,
        "text": "The tiktoken library is the tokenizer library used by OpenAI, and it converts a sentence into token IDs.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@543",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@545",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@548",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@550",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@552",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@554"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p020",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "definition",
        "importance": 2,
        "text": "Byte pair encoding is a subword tokenizer, so a word is not necessarily equal to one token.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@554",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@558",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@559",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@562",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@564",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@567",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@569",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@572"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p021",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "The tokenizer used here is the byte pair encoder tokenizer that GPT models use, and it converts each text into token IDs.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@576",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@578",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@580",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@583",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@585",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@587",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@590",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@593",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@595"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p022",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "procedure",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "After tokenization, shorter token sequences are padded with token 50256 until all sequences have the same length.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@600",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@602",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@604",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@607",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@609",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@611",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@613",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@616",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@618",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@621"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p023",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "definition",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "In the GPT-2 vocabulary, token 50256 is the end-of-text token.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@631",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@633",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@635",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@637",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@640",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@642",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@645",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@648",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@650",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@654",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@656",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@659",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@660",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@662"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p024",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "definition",
        "importance": 2,
        "text": "The GPT-2 vocabulary maps tokens to token IDs.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@631",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@633",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@635",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@637",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@640",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@680",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@681",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@685",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@687",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@689",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@692",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@694"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p025",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 2,
        "text": "The end-of-text token signifies that the end of a document or sentence has been reached and is used during GPT training to distinguish between separate document sources.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@662",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@665",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@668",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@669",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@672",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@675",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@677"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p026",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "analogy",
        "importance": 1,
        "text": "The vocabulary can be thought of like a dictionary in which words have associated meanings, except here words have associated token IDs.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@694",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@697",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@700",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@702",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@704",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@706"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p027",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 2,
        "text": "Although any token could be used for padding, using the end-of-text token makes symbolic sense because it indicates there is no additional text and avoids confusing the model with a random word.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@722",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@725",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@727",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@730",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@732",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@736",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@738",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@740",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@743",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@745"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p028",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "procedure",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "The overall workflow is to take the train, validation, and test CSV text, tokenize it into token IDs, find the longest email, and pad all messages with end-of-text token 50256 to that same length.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@751",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@754",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@756",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@758",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@763",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@765",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@767",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@769",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@771",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@773",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@775",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@778",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@781"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p029",
        "video_id": "f6zqClXOh7Y",
        "lecture": 34,
        "kind": "definition",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "Before instantiating a DataLoader, a PyTorch Dataset must be implemented to specify how the data is loaded and processed.",
        "line_ids": [
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@793",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@795",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@798",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@801",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@802",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@805",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@807",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@810",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@811",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@813",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@816",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@818",
          "f6zqClXOh7Y@820"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6zqClXOh7Y:p030",
